WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.780
Narrator: Why study
alone, when you can study

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:06.030
with a bunch of strangers
from all over the world?

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.280
This is gongbang, which translates

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.760
to "study broadcast" in Korean.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.320
In Japan, it's known as benkyou douga,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.200
and in the US and other
parts of the world,

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.590
it's simply called "study with me."

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.650
It's a type of video in which
people prepare for an exam

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.760
on camera, while other people
watch and study as well.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.540
This might not sound like
riveting entertainment,

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:30.980
but these videos are racking up

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.720
millions of views on YouTube.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.430
So why are they so popular?

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.770
And is this just the
latest example of lazy

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.673
content creation, or is there
something more going on here?

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.590
Narrator: This YouTuber goes

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.460
by the name "The Man Sitting Next to Me."

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.110
He prefers to remain anonymous.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.230
He lives in Korea and is
studying to be a tax accountant.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.250
He livestreams himself
studying for hours at a time

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.893
and has over 20 thousand
subscribers on his channel.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:19.773
His videos show him
simply sitting at a desk

00:01:19.773 --> 00:01:20.606
taking notes or looking at a book.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.770
Jamie Lee: You're studying
for a long time whether it's

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.930
for high school, college,
or a professional degree.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.170
It can be really lonely,
and it's always helpful

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.590
to have sort of a study buddy.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.210
Narrator: This is Jamie.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.480
She lives in New York and runs

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.480
a YouTube channel called
"The Strive to Fit."

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.770
She started her channel when she was

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.570
in medical school and continues

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.130
to film herself studying
while in residency.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.980
Unlike The Man Sitting Next to Me,

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.160
Jamie's "study with me"
videos are prerecorded.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.430
Her videos range from around 20 minutes

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:54.910
to two-and-a-half hours in length,

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:57.820
and some have over two million views.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.030
She offers videos with music and some

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.700
with the natural sounds of the pencil

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.880
on paper as a sort of ASMR.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.780
Jamie: I try to cater to
different types of audiences.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.340
It'll just be kind of the
natural sound of like,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.090
pen and paper or book
sounds and things like that.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:18.310
Narrator: With a quick YouTube search,

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.320
you can find all sorts of different people

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.520
and types of study videos to choose from.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.640
This study video trend
is a lot like mukbang,

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.340
which has become more
popular in recent years.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.300
It involves people eating food,

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.740
often a whole lot of it, on camera.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.720
Looking at what's on YouTube these days,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.150
like ASMR, mukbang, and reaction videos,

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.480
it would seem like you don't need

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.220
a whole lot of special
skills to be a success.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.670
But gongbang serves a real
purpose, helping people focus.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.810
Or at least that's what
the content creator

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.570
and the fans are saying.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.770
While it may seem
distracting to watch YouTube

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.460
while doing your work,
they say it actually

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.573
is a helpful way to avoid distractions.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.070
Narrator: And based on
some of the comments,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.177
this method seems to be working.

00:03:21.177 --> 00:03:24.027
"I'm an engineering student,
a week ago I wasn't motivated

00:03:24.027 --> 00:03:26.337
at all, then I found your channel.

00:03:26.337 --> 00:03:28.457
Thank you for saving me."

00:03:28.457 --> 00:03:31.457
"I feel more concentrated
on my exam preparation.

00:03:31.457 --> 00:03:33.457
Thank you Jamie for your video.

00:03:33.457 --> 00:03:35.837
It's actually very nice and helpful."

00:03:36.889 --> 00:03:39.740
Jamie: It really helps them
focus, because every time

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.410
they're tempted to do
something on their phone

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.180
or go somewhere on their website,

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.210
they kinda look up and see
that I'm still studying

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.380
on the screen, so it
kind of motivates them

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.450
to keep studying and stay focused

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.080
on what they're supposed to be doing.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.950
Narrator: And while they do
make money off the videos

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.720
with YouTube ads, they say viral fame

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.770
and fortune isn't why they're doing it.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.150
Keep in mind, these YouTubers are studying

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.650
for careers outside of YouTube,

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.460
like tax accountants and doctors.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:08.850
They say they're only using YouTube

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.530
as a means to an end, that isn't becoming

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.440
the next Logan Paul or Jenna Marbles.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:15.840
The Man Sitting Next to Me says

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.950
he even uses the money that he earns

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.160
to give back to his community,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.280
charities, and nursing homes.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.970
You can find him on
his channel interacting

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.070
with his viewers, giving out gift cards

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.483
for things like coffee
and books to help study.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.250
Narrator: His followers will often stick

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.500
with him until they complete their exam

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.250
and share his channel with friends.

00:05:17.013 --> 00:05:17.846
Narrator: But what happens

00:05:17.846 --> 00:05:20.280
when the tests are over
for these YouTubers?

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.600
What can their followers expect?

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.420
Both Jamie and The Man Sitting Next to Me

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.910
say they plan to keep filming themselves

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.910
but perhaps with different
content related to their careers.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.170
Narrator: No matter why you
watch or when you watch,

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:50.530
it all comes down to one thing:

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.773
making sure you do the
work and pass that test.

